@page "/sourcery/making-languages-in-rust-chapter-2"
@using Sorcery.ServerSide.Components.Blogging;
@using Sorcery.ServerSide.Components.Footnotes;
@inject BlogBook BlogBook;

@code {
    public static readonly RenderFragment Introduction = __builder =>
    {
        <Paragraph>
            In this chapter we create a custom MLIR dialect for our Toy language and implement codegen
            from the AST to the high-level dialect operations. We also start the fun&trade; part
            &ndash; Rust/C++ interop.
        </Paragraph>
    };
}

<BlogPost Post="BlogBook.MakingLanguagesInRust.Posts[2]">
    @Introduction
    
    <Paragraph>
        Custom dialects are what MLIR is all about. The MLIR infrastructure is designed to accommodate essentially
        arbitrary IR extensions, as long as they are in SSA form<Footnote>
            Static Single Assignment. Essentially, every value has a single place (lexically) where it is defined.
            This makes things way easier for transformations on the IR later on.
        </Footnote>. A custom IR is called a <Highlight>dialect</Highlight> and the core unit in their construction
        is an <Highlight>operation</Highlight>. 
    </Paragraph>
</BlogPost>
